 ABSTRACT:
 Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) devices continuously transmit patient vital signs, making them attractive targets for False Data Injection (FDI) attacks that falsify sensor readings and threaten patient safety. Existing physiology-aware detection methods validate each physiological channel independently against plausible medical ranges, leaving a blind spot: a coordinated adversary can manipulate multiple correlated vital-sign channels simultaneously, keeping each individual reading physiologically plausible while the combination violates known relationships between vital signs. This coordinated multi-channel FDI threat has received limited attention and remains insufficiently addressed in existing IoMT security literature. To address this gap, the project proposes a Cross-Sensor Consistency Modeling (CSCM) framework that jointly analyzes relationships among vital-sign channels using a physiology-aware graph attention approach informed by known clinical correlations. The framework will be implemented and evaluated on public ICU and wearable physiological datasets, including PhysioNet/CinC-2012, MIMIC-III Waveform, and WESAD, using physiological variables available in each dataset, and benchmarked against single-channel baselines to assess effectiveness against coordinated attacks while preserving performance on conventional scenarios. This research aims to contribute: (i) a coordinated multi-channel FDI attack model, (ii) a physiology-aware CSCM framework for coordinated attack detection, and (iii) an experimental evaluation across multiple public healthcare datasets, addressing an underexplored security vulnerability in medical IoT networks.
