 [12] SUMMARY:
 The proposed project, "Coordinated Multi-Channel False Data Injection Attack Detection Using Physiology-Aware Cross-Sensor Consistency Modeling in IoMT," focuses on improving the security and reliability of physiological data transmitted by Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) devices. Medical monitoring systems continuously collect vital signs such as heart rate, oxygen saturation, blood pressure, respiratory rate, and temperature, making these systems potential targets for False Data Injection (FDI) attacks. Existing detection approaches often evaluate individual sensor channels independently or focus mainly on network-level intrusions. This creates a potential vulnerability where an attacker can simultaneously manipulate multiple correlated physiological signals while keeping each individual value within a medically plausible range. Although the manipulated values may appear normal separately, their combined pattern may violate expected physiological relationships. The project therefore aims to formally model this coordinated multi-channel FDI threat and experimentally evaluate the vulnerability of existing detection approaches using real physiological datasets.
 To address this limitation, the project proposes a Cross-Sensor Consistency Modeling (CSCM) framework that jointly analyzes relationships among multiple physiological channels. The system will preprocess public physiological datasets such as PhysioNet/CinC-2012, WESAD, and MIMIC-III Waveform where accessible, generate controlled coordinated FDI attacks, and compare the proposed approach with appropriate baseline detection methods. A physiology-informed graph attention network will represent relationships between vital-sign channels and learn patterns of cross-sensor consistency for attack detection. The proposed system will be evaluated using metrics such as sensitivity, specificity, precision, F1-score, false-positive rate, and AUROC, together with statistical significance testing, ablation studies, and attention-based interpretability analysis. The expected outcome is a reproducible detection framework capable of identifying coordinated attacks that may evade independent sensor-level validation while maintaining performance against conventional attacks. The project aims to contribute a coordinated multi-channel attack model, a physiology-aware cross-sensor detection framework, and a comprehensive experimental evaluation for improving the security and reliability of IoMT physiological monitoring systems.
